A||||21||Adrift||Widening suspension Methods?||||5||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-04-2001||01:36 AM||||Ok, to bring the wheels out farther for a ferrari replica i've been told of two methods: Wheelspacers (unsafe) and the Held motorsport widetracksuspension(http://www.heldmotorsports.com/wide.htm - Very expensive, especially for a Canadian!).<P>So here's my question, are there any other ways that I can widen my suspension? (Maybe build the held kit myself somehow? How would I go about doing that? Can it be done cheaper?) ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DKOV||12-04-2001||02:14 AM||||Doesn't JEGS sell a "suspension stretch tool"?  Just take off the wheel, bolt the stretch tool to the hub and pull the crank.  I pull for every inch you need.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||12-04-2001||03:30 AM||||No, it can't be done any cheaper.  You could have a race shop make you a one-off, but it'd be untested and it'd cost $10,000+ US.<P>BUY THE HELD SETUP.   It's not only the best, it's ALL there is.<P>Or buy a casket.  That simple :S)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||RedOktober||12-04-2001||03:43 AM||||What type of kit do you have???<P>if its the 355, pretty much you will use a 4" wheel spacer. Its been done before and as long as you are not racing it is fine. <P>If you are doing a TR, then Compomotive makes some killer wide offset wheels in 16 and 17 that will work with the stock5x100mm bolt pattern.<P>my only question is: I'm sure you have spent a small fortune on building your kit, WHY try to start saving money when you get to the wheels. The proper offset wheel and tire package will cost anywhere between 2500-3800 Cnd.<P>Hope this helps<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-04-2001||07:14 AM||||go with held kit. there lengthened control arms and axles. Your wheels then can be OEM looking. Id get big offsets before Id ever use adapters, but theyll look 'phoney' and put a hell of a load on wheel hubs/bearings. Ive seen adapters break just driving on a normal road, expecially when there that wide.||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||12-04-2001||11:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RedOktober:<BR><B>What type of kit do you have???<P>if its the 355, pretty much you will use a 4" wheel spacer. Its been done before and as long as you are not racing it is fine. <BR>Hope this helps</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Ah no its not fine, well as long as you dont drive it a leave it parked in the driveway, then its fine.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark Matthews||12-04-2001||11:51 AM||||Wheel spacer question if I end up doing the Jalapeno.  PISA says they use a 1" wheel adapter to change the bolt pattern and the wheels have a 3.5" back spacing.  How steardy is this setup, since they're trying to say it's an offroad vehicle.<P>The 4" seems insane for a spacer.  I've never heard of the 1" adapters, are they strong?<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||1
Z||000007||ITB 2M4||12-04-2001||01:45 PM||||When my dad and I raced a '74 Corolla SR-5, we used a 1/2 inch wheel spacer with no problmes what-so-ever.  The car weighed 1950lbs. however, and we were using some 18lb., 13 inch wheels from Speedway Direct.  I wouldn't go any more than 1/2 inch, which probably wouldn't get you the width you're looking for.<BR>The other possibility is to go to a fabrication shop in your area.  Take them the A-arms, and tell them how much you need added to them.  The problem with this is, they would almost have to have an engineering degree to make sure your bump steer and wheel clearance wasn't adversely affected.  Probably more trouble than it's worth, IMO.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||10
Z||000008||DKOV||12-04-2001||02:04 PM||||Fact is, wheel spacers push the wheel farther off the supporting bearings and tranfer more torsional weight into the bearings as opposed to "past" them.  As such, premature bearing wear occurs.  Sure, if you do not race the car with 4 inch spacers it won't fall apart AS FAST, but it will stress the bearings beyond their tolerances even under normal driving.<P>Think of it like a really tough bolt you are trying to remove...  When you have a short wrench or ratchet, you have little or no leverage to break that thing loose.  The LONGER the handles (farther away from the bolt) you get, the more stress you put on that bolt resulting in better leverage and more stress on the bolt with much less pressure.<P>Even 1 inch spacers will provide and increase in wear, albeit less than 4 inches.<P>I have a 355 kit and I am using the HELD wide track suspension.  Four inches per side in the rear and two inches per side in the front.<P>Let's be realisitc, even though many of us may never take their car onto a track, it is rediculous to believe we will never put our foot in it or take a corner "at speed".  C'mon.<P>If you are building a Jalapeno, the stresses of taking it offroad at all, will be equally great and damaging as a trip around a track.<P>Don't spend you time and money on building a beautiful project, then find yourself hiding it in the garage due to bad bearings because you tried to save some bucks.  Build it right and get out there and DRIVE IT!!!  Show the world just how cool these cars can be!<P>JMHO...<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000009||VISCERAL||12-04-2001||05:13 PM||||rcc homepage<BR> <A HREF="http://www.dwayne9.addr.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dwayne9.addr.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spyder||12-06-2001||12:15 AM||||You are doing the same thing I did when I first started with my build on deciding what is best. <BR>I went overboard with most everything on this car. I wish I would have built her more simple, I would have been driving her last year.<BR>Go with spacers and save money and get the car on the road...<BR>The width of the spacers depends on the offset of the wheel.  I have a V8 in my car and only using a TWO inch spacers on the rear, the rear wheels are 9.5" wide with 275s on the wheels. I have wider spacers on the front than the rear....<BR>The total width from outside the rear tire to rear outside of the other tire is 78 inches  <IMG SRC="http://markalexandermiller.com/1aredc.JPG"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||DKOV||12-06-2001||10:11 AM||||Spyder...<P>I remember those pics...  Havn't you got any NEW pics yet?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Also, I figured you would have gone and purchased the real tails instead of those.  They look good though.<P>When you say "all out" on the project, I understand the disease...  I even went as far as scavanging a discarded 355 interior to adapt to my fiero!  Had to buy some parts from the Ferrari Dealership to complete but...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Your car still looks great!!!  Good job!!!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000012||Daleford||12-06-2001||11:55 AM||||You think you have problems! I'm installing a Koenig version of the Testarossa which is about 8 inches wider than the Testa!  Add to that, we are using a Cadillac 4-speed automatic transaxel.  No one makes any widening parts for that. <BR>Fortunately we have found a source for longer axels that fit between the CV joints. I plan to extend the lower control arm at the ball joint area and move the upper strut mount outward. I still will need custom wheels at that point. Wish I could find a source for wheel shells [rim section only] and centers. That way you can set up whatever offset you need and weld your own. Anybody know of any?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000013||artherd||12-06-2001||04:09 PM||||Most 3-piece wheel makers will custom-make you a set in *ANY* offset you desire.<P>HRE is an example; <A HREF="http://www.hrewheels.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hrewheels.com</A> <P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Daleford:<BR><B>You think you have problems! I'm installing a Koenig version of the Testarossa which is about 8 inches wider than the Testa!  Add to that, we are using a Cadillac 4-speed automatic transaxel.  No one makes any widening parts for that. <BR>Fortunately we have found a source for longer axels that fit between the CV joints. I plan to extend the lower control arm at the ball joint area and move the upper strut mount outward. I still will need custom wheels at that point. Wish I could find a source for wheel shells [rim section only] and centers. That way you can set up whatever offset you need and weld your own. Anybody know of any?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
